  "status": "All six native jobs and both actual WASI regression jobs pass",
  "workload": "UDP socket lifetimes through original function scopes, aliases, closure/record holders, cancelled tasks and two simultaneous receivers; 4096 real bind/drop cycles, 301 aliases, constructor failure, every one of three managed receive allocations, and real shared-library calls/unload while a host socket stays open. TCP/UDP addresses containing NUL are rejected before prefix use. Invalid compiler options produce no artifact.",
  "validation": "Raw and optimized interpreter outputs match O1/O2 native tracing stress/reuse and checked ARC. Physical-free-disabled tracing preserves logical socket cleanup. ARC binaries and libraries exclude tracing symbols. Native cleanup controls deliberately omit descriptor, partial-field, wait-list and library-finalizer cleanup; each must fail. Header size equals the previous socket representation, with aligned UDP counters sharing the TLS payload slot under the socket-kind tag. A missing-tag destructor control fails. Library calls repeat 32 times per load across three load/unload cycles; tracing uses both reuse-poison settings, ARC physically frees and explicitly rejects reuse-poison mode.",
  "limits": "No throughput, latency, allocation-traffic or C/Rust performance claim. Zero final storage means measured UDP values/scratch and UDP descriptors return to their initialized baseline; persistent scheduler state, the epoll descriptor and allocator reservations are not zero process memory. Weak finalizers remain for unproved sharing in tracing builds. Opaque closure holders are scoped conservatively, which can inhibit optimization. Native sockets are not supported WASI networking. WASI runs include actual modules plus native collector-free allocator controls; compiler/runtime ownership sources match the native head, while later CLI/test changes require full production validation. Retained artifacts cover executable lifetime/cleanup probes; library/tag/CLI outcomes are in CI test results rather than separate archived binaries.",
